so consider the constitution rule about splitting implementation and tests—though the SRD requirements might already account for both aspects, I need to think about how to structure these tasks effectively.\n\nI don't actually have the SRD content to see what R4.1-R4.4 specifically cover, so I should reference the actual SRD file at `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd026-cut.yaml` so the stitch agent can pull the details. Let me sketch out the task structure: Task 0 would handle cut's R4.1-R4.4 requirements (weight check: 1+1+1+1 = 4, which fits), and Task 1 would cover paste's R1.1-R1.4 (also weight 4 total). I'm realizing the constitution requires splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks, and the requirement groups likely follow a pattern where R1-R3 handle implementation aspects and R4 covers testing. Since I don't have the actual SRD structure, I should group the paste requirements by their R-group (R1, R2, R3, R4) as each probably represents a distinct logical unit that maps to one task.\n\nFor paste specifically, all 14 requirements are ready and untouched, so I need to figure out how to distribute them across available task slots—with 14 requirements and a 4-requirement limit per task, I'd need at least 4 tasks, but I'm constrained by the total slots available.\n\nI'm settling on a structure where cut gets one task for R4.1-R4.4, and paste gets three tasks covering R1-R3 groups sequentially, with each task depending on the previous one. The question is whether task 1 for paste should depend on task 0 (cut) or task -1 (something earlier).\n\nSince cut and paste are independent utilities, task 1 can stand alone with no dependency. Tasks 2 and 3 then build on task 1 since they're all part of the same paste package.\n\nSo my final breakdown is: task 0 handles the remaining cut requirements with no dependency, task 1 covers the first batch of paste requirements independently, then task 2 and 3 each build on the previous paste task. That leaves the 