ecking that the requirements I'm assigning add up correctly across all three paste tasks. I'm verifying the resource constraints are satisfied across all tasks, then I need to finalize the output format with proper YAML structure. But I'm second-guessing whether these tasks are substantial enough—the P2 rule suggests 250-350 lines touching multiple files, yet some of these feel smaller in scope. Still, the P6 constraint is clear: map each SRD requirement group to exactly one task without crossing boundaries, and the sub-requirement limit caps tasks at 4 items each. The metrics about acceptance criteria and design decisions are secondary to these hard constraints, so my task breakdown should hold. Let me write up the final response with all the required fields. For paste, I'm mapping out the requirement groups: R1 covers the core parallel merge functionality with multiple file reads and delimiter joining, R2 handles the serial mode flag and cycling delimiters, R3 addresses stdin and file input handling plus error cases, and R4 focuses on differential testing and edge cases. Now I need to write the YAML with task descriptions that are self-contained—each one should give the stitch agent enough context to execute without needing my analysis, either by including the details directly or pointing to the specific SRD requirement IDs they need to reference. files to read.\n\nHere's my final plan:\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"cmd/cut -- srd026 R4.1-R4.4 (differential tests and error handling)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd026-cut.yaml (R4.1 through R4.4)\n      - cmd/cut/main.go (existing implementation to test against)\n      - pkg/testutils/ (DiffTest and RunDiffTests framework)\n      - CLAUDE.md (differential testing pattern and SIGPIPE requirements)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/cut/cut_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Differential tests for cut using testutils.RunDiffTests\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement srd026-cut requirement R4.1 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement srd026-cut requirement R4.2 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement srd026-cut requirement R4.3 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement srd026-cut requirement R4.4 as specified in the SRD\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Use testutils.BuildBinary(t, \\\".\\\") to compile cmd/cut and exec.LookPath(\\\"gcut\\\") for the reference binary\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Skip test with t.Skip if reference binary g\n\nI'm laying out the requirements for each of the four SRD sections I need to test, then establishing the design approach for how to build and invoke both the implementation and reference binaries in the differential tests. I'm realizing the task description needs to be more concrete than just referencing requirement numbers—the stitch agent should understand what actually needs to be implemented without having to look up the SRD separately. For the cut utility, I should describe the specific functionality like field/byte/character selection with various flags, delimiter handling, and output formatting, rather than leaving it vague. Same approach for paste and other utilities—spell out what the implementation should cover so the task is genuinely self-contained. I'm realizing I need to reference the actual SRD file since I can't infer the precise R4 requirements for cut from just the release notes. Rather than guess at the specifics, I'll structure the requirements to point the stitch agent to the SRD document where the detailed specifications live, since they'll need to read it anyway for implementation details.\n\nLooking at the requirement structure across both SRDs, I notice paste has a fuller set of ready requirements across all R-groups compared to cut which only has R4.1-R4.4 marked ready. The naming convention and architecture placement suggest these are related operations, so I should consider whether the task breakdown makes sense to keep them separate or if there's a better way to organize this.\n\nWith the size constraints, I could fit cut's four requirements into one task, then allocate paste's requirements across the remaining three tasks by grouping R-groups. The question is whether combining R2 and R3 from paste would work better than keeping them separate, since both have three requirements each.\n\nActually, combining them would exceed the four-requirement limit, so that won't work. And mixing sub-requirements from different R-groups violates the boundary rule, even though the constraint technically applies to individual sub-requirements rather than groups. The P6 rule seems to reinforce keeping R-groups intact within tasks.\n\nSo my current approach of mapping each complete R-group to its own task is the right call. Now I need to finalize the output with weight checks and the YAML structure.\n\nBut there's something odd here—the code_status shows \"not started\" for cut, yet R1-R3 aren't marked as \"ready\" in requirement_states. That's contradictory. If no code exists, they should be ready. Maybe they were just transitioned out of ready status?\n\nLooking at the git history, task #5283 was working on R1-R3 and has already completed and merged. So by the time the stitch agent tackles R4, the R1-R3 implementation should already exist. The task description needs to account for this dependency gracefully.\n\nFor the cut feature, R1-R3 are done and R4.1-R4.4 are ready for the next task. For paste, all requirements are still ready since no work has been started yet. Since the cut R1-R3 code is already in place from the merged task, the R4 task can build on that existing implementation.\n\nNow I need to consider the ordering constraint about documentation before code and implementation before tests. Since cut R4 involves testing and